WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.029
Faster models, fast mode, and precomputation.

2
00:00:03.309 --> 00:00:05.955
Smaller models generate faster per token.

3
00:00:06.235 --> 00:00:16.965
The cookbook measured output speed on its workload at about 581 tokens per second for Haiku, 236 for Sonnet, and 153 for Opus.

4
00:00:17.245 --> 00:00:21.789
But per-task time is what matters, and it measured Sonnet’s median task at 3.

5
00:00:22.069 --> 00:00:24.181
5 seconds and Haiku’s at 4.

6
00:00:24.461 --> 00:00:28.536
0, because the smaller model wrote more tokens and accuracy fell.

7
00:00:28.816 --> 00:00:35.515
A faster model is not automatically a faster task, and switching models is a migration: re-run the eval.

8
00:00:35.795 --> 00:00:40.040
Fast mode runs the same model with a faster inference configuration.

9
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.283
Anthropic’s docs list up to 2.

10
00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:50.328
5 times higher output tokens per second, focused on output speed rather than TTFT, on Claude Opus 5.

11
00:00:50.608 --> 00:00:59.483
5 (generally available) and a few other models, at a premium (8 and 40 dollars per million input and output tokens on Opus 5.

12
00:00:59.763 --> 00:01:02.707
5 at the time of writing, twice the standard rate).

13
00:01:02.987 --> 00:01:15.936
It has its own rate limit, isn’t available with the Batch API, and requests at different speeds don’t share cached prefixes, so falling back to standard speed on a 429 is also a cache miss.

14
00:01:16.216 --> 00:01:24.813
In the cookbook it cut median and p90 task time by 53 to 59 percent at every effort level, at roughly double the cost.

15
00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:31.301
When you report a paid lever like this, always show beside it what the no-cost changes achieved on their own.

16
00:01:31.581 --> 00:01:34.952
Precompute whatever doesn’t need to happen while the user waits.

17
00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:44.832
OpenAI’s guide ends its list with “don’t default to an LLM”: hard-coded responses, precomputed outputs, and ordinary caching where they fit.

18
00:01:45.112 --> 00:02:04.248
In practice: generate summaries and classifications in a nightly batch job (half price, lesson 5) and serve them from your database; cache final answers in your own application keyed by a normalized input, with an expiry and an invalidation rule; and pre-warm the prompt cache before traffic arrives.

19
00:02:04.528 --> 00:02:20.485
Anthropic’s caching docs describe sending a request with max_tokens set to 0, which writes the cache without generating output; the cookbook measured a first turn on a 146,000-token prompt falling from 1.

20
00:02:20.765 --> 00:02:21.981
0 to 0.

21
00:02:22.261 --> 00:02:23.563
8 seconds this way.

22
00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:31.373
Pre-warming costs a cache write, so it pays only when the first request’s latency is user-visible and the prefix is large.
